<%# Fuime: the pricing lesson.

    Teaches a method and names no number. §8.3 D2's mitigation for worker
    misclassification is that operators set their own rates and Fuime never does,
    so the single most useful sentence this page could contain is the one it may
    not write. What it can do is make sure the reader is not left guessing
    instead, which is what "no suggested rate" degrades into if nothing replaces
    it. spec/requests/learn_spec.rb asserts both halves. %>

<p>
  Nobody can tell you what to charge, including us. Your costs, your speed, your
  area and your competition are all different from everyone else's, and a number
  copied from somebody with different costs is a number that quietly loses you
  money.
</p>

<p>
  What you can do is work it out on purpose. Here is the method.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Step one: find your floor</h2>

<p>
  Your floor is the number below which the job is not worth doing. Work this out
  first, before you think about anyone else, because it is the only part of
  pricing that is a fact rather than an opinion.
</p>

<p>Add up, for one job:</p>

<ul class="m-0 pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
  <li>Everything the job consumes. Materials, fuel, printing, packaging, postage.</li>
  <li>Wear on your equipment. If a set of blades lasts thirty jobs, a thirtieth of them belongs to this one.</li>
  <li>
    Every hour the job takes. Not the visible hour. Travel both ways, setting up,
    editing afterwards, and the messages before and after. A one hour job is
    almost never one hour.
  </li>
  <li>Whatever the payment costs you, since taking money is never entirely free.</li>
</ul>

<p>
  Now decide what an hour of your time is worth to you. This is the one genuinely
  personal input, and it is the one people leave out. Leave it out and you will
  work all summer and discover you did it for less than you would have accepted
  from anybody else.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Step two: find the ceiling</h2>

<p>
  Get a real quote from an established business that does the same thing near
  you. Not a guess, an actual quote. That is roughly the most anybody would pay,
  and it tells you how much room you have.
</p>

<p>
  You are allowed to sit below it. You are not obliged to sit far below it. The
  reflex to price at the very bottom because you are young is the most common
  and most expensive mistake on this page, and it does something worse than lose
  you money: it tells the customer your work is worth less. Some of them will
  believe you.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Step three: pick a number and test it</h2>

<p>
  Your first price is a guess with reasoning behind it, which is fine. What
  matters is what you do next.
</p>

<p>
  Raise the price for the <em>next</em> new customer, not for the people you
  already have. If nobody hesitates, you were too low, so do it again. Keep going
  until people start pausing. That pause is real information about what your work
  is worth, and there is no way to get it except by going slightly too far once.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Four things that are not a pricing method</h2>

<ul class="m-0 pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
  <li>Matching a friend's number without knowing their costs.</li>
  <li>Charging what feels polite.</li>
  <li>Dropping the number because somebody pulled a face. Silence after a price is normal. Wait it out.</li>
  <li>Competing on being the cheapest. There is always somebody willing to go lower, and you cannot win a race to the bottom, you can only finish it.</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Charge for the job, not the hour</h2>

<p>
  Once you know how long something takes, quote the whole job. If you charge by
  the hour, getting faster makes you less money, which is a strange thing to
  build a business on. Quote the job and getting better pays you directly.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Raising prices on people you already have</h2>

<p>
  It is normal and every business does it. Tell them before it happens, in
  writing, with a date, and give a short reason. Let anyone who wants to leave
  go without an argument. Most will stay. Springing it on somebody halfway
  through a job costs you the customer and the recommendation.
</p>

<div class="card">
  <p class="m-0 text-sm">
    <strong>One quiet trick:</strong> offer three options rather than one, at
    different sizes. Most people pick the middle one, and you learn more from
    which one they pick than from a yes or a no.
  </p>
</div>
